        content: "Accounting standards ensure consistency, reliability, comparability, and transparency in financial reporting.\n\nGoing Concern: Assumes the business will continue operating for the foreseeable future.\nImplication: Assets valued at cost (less depreciation), NOT break-up/liquidation value. Prepayments recognised. Long-term liabilities classified separately.\nExample: Machine NBV €35,000, break-up value €20,000. Under going concern: €35,000.\nIf NOT going concern: All assets at fire-sale values, all debts immediately payable, auditor qualifies the report.\nIn doubt when: Continued losses, cannot pay debts, lost major customers, bank withdrew overdraft.\n\nAccruals (Matching) Concept: Revenue and expenses recognised in the period they RELATE TO, not when cash moves.\nTwo aspects:\n1. Revenue recognised when EARNED (delivery/service), not when cash received\n2. Expenses matched to the revenue they helped generate\n\nApplications: Accruals (expense incurred not paid = still an expense this year), Prepayments (expense paid for next year = excluded), Depreciation (cost spread over useful life), Closing stock (deducted from CoS — relates to next year's revenue).\n\nExample: €12,000 rent paid 1 Oct for Oct-Mar (6 months). Year end 31 Dec. This year's expense = €6,000 (3 months). Prepayment = €6,000 (current asset).",
